+# Mobile WalletConnect deep-linking on "Stake"
+
+## TL;DR
+
+Two independent things happen when you sign on mobile WC: (a) the request is
+**published to the relay** so it queues inside the wallet, and (b) the browser
+tries to **foreground the wallet app**. (a) always works — that's why the
+request is "waiting" in Rainbow. (b) is what's broken. The fix is to publish
+first, then deep-link a beat later — never the reverse.
+
+---
+
+## (1) Why nothing auto-opened before the patch
+
+When your dApp is connected to Rainbow over **WalletConnect v2**, Rainbow is a
+separate app. `writeContractAsync` does not talk to Rainbow directly; it hands
+an `eth_sendTransaction` request to the WalletConnect provider, which:
+
+1. serializes + encrypts the request and **publishes it to the WC relay
+   server** (a network round-trip). The wallet, subscribed to that topic,
+   receives it and shows the signing prompt. This is why, when the user
+   manually switches to Rainbow, the request is already sitting there — the
+   relay leg works fine.
+2. tries to **redirect the user to the wallet** so they actually see the
+   prompt. RainbowKit / WC does this using the wallet's redirect info stored on
+   the session (`session.peer.metadata.redirect.native` /
+   `.universal`), i.e. it opens the wallet's deep link.
+
+Step 2 is the flaky part, and it fails for a mobile-browser reason:
+
+- **Opening another app requires a live user activation (the tap).** iOS Safari
+  (and mobile Chrome) only let a page navigate to an external app scheme while a
+  "transient user activation" from the tap is still valid.
+- `writeContractAsync` is **async** — encrypt + publish to the relay is a
+  network round-trip. By the time WC is ready to redirect, the `await` has
+  already unwound the call stack and the tap's activation has been consumed /
+  expired. The browser then **silently refuses** to open the app.
+- (Secondary cause: if the connected wallet didn't populate `redirect` on its
+  session metadata, RainbowKit has no target to open and does nothing.)
+
+Net: request lands in the wallet, but the browser never brings the wallet
+forward, so the page "just sits there."
+
+---
+
+## (2) Why the patch made it worse
+
+```js
+window.location.href = "rainbow://";   // line 1
+await writeContractAsync(...);          // line 2
+```
+
+This opens Rainbow **before the request exists**:
+
+- `window.location.href = "rainbow://"` fires immediately, before
+  `writeContractAsync` has built, encrypted, or published anything. Rainbow
+  comes forward with **nothing queued** → "nothing to sign."
+- Worse, navigating away **backgrounds the web page**. Mobile browsers throttle
+  or suspend JS in a backgrounded tab, so the subsequent `writeContractAsync` —
+  the thing that publishes the request — may never run to completion. The relay
+  publish is paused/dropped, so the tx **never lands onchain**.
+
+So the patch traded "wallet doesn't open" for "wallet opens empty and the tx is
+never sent" — strictly worse.
+
+---
+
+## (3) The correct pattern
+
+**Order: publish first, deep-link second, with a short delay in between.**
+
+```js
+async function onStake() {
+  // 1. Kick off the write — this triggers the WC relay publish.
+  const txPromise = writeContractAsync({ /* ... */ });
+
+  // 2. Give WC time to encrypt + publish to the relay, THEN foreground wallet.
+  if (shouldDeepLink()) {
+    setTimeout(() => {
+      window.location.href = getWalletRedirect(); // from session metadata
+    }, 200); // ~150–300ms
+  }
+
+  await txPromise;
+}
+```
+
+### The ordering and the delay
+
+- Start the write **first** so the request is actually created and pushed to the
+  relay. Do **not** `await` it before redirecting — you'd never open the wallet
+  in time. Fire the redirect on a short timer while the promise runs.
+- The delay exists to cover the **relay publish round-trip**: long enough that
+  by the time the wallet is foregrounded the request is already queued (too
+  short = empty wallet, same bug as the patch), but short enough to stay inside
+  the browser's transient-activation window (~5s on iOS) and feel instant. A
+  few hundred ms (~150–300ms) covers typical relay latency with margin.
+- Better than a fixed timer: redirect off the WC provider's own "request sent"
+  signal if you can hook it. The timer is the pragmatic version.
+- Best of all: prefer RainbowKit's **built-in** redirect handling (it already
+  knows the connected wallet and its links) over hand-rolling this. The manual
+  deep link is a fallback for when the automatic one doesn't fire.
+
+### Which scheme to open — do NOT hardcode `rainbow://`
+
+`connector.id === "walletConnect"` tells you only that the transport is
+WalletConnect, **not which wallet** the user picked. Hardcoding `rainbow://`
+breaks MetaMask, Trust, etc. Read the target from the **active session's peer
+metadata**:
+
+- `session.peer.metadata.redirect.universal` → prefer this (an https universal
+  link): it opens the app if installed and falls back to the App Store /
+  browser if not, and doesn't throw a scary "open in app?" prompt.
+- `session.peer.metadata.redirect.native` → the custom scheme
+  (`rainbow://`, `metamask://`, …), use as fallback.
+- `session.peer.metadata.name` identifies the wallet if you need to branch.
+
+If no redirect metadata is present, don't guess a scheme — let RainbowKit handle
+it or leave the user to switch manually.
+
+### When to skip the deep link entirely
+
+- **Not mobile** — desktop browser: nothing to open; a scheme navigation errors
+  or no-ops.
+- **Injected / extension connector** (`connector.id !== "walletConnect"`, e.g.
+  injected MetaMask, Coinbase) — the wallet lives in-page; no app to foreground.
+- **Inside a wallet's in-app dApp browser** (already running in Rainbow /
+  MetaMask browser) — an injected provider is present; deep-linking would bounce
+  you out or loop.
+- **Desktop WC via QR** — the approval happens on the user's phone; the desktop
+  page has no app to open.
+- **No active WC session / no redirect metadata** — no valid target to open.
+
+Guard the redirect on: mobile device **and** `connector.id === "walletConnect"`
+**and** no injected provider in-page **and** a redirect target exists.

